332.m:
Vegavatī is taught as sa, sa, ma, gā, and bha, bha, bha, go, ga;
[Footnote]
[Variant readings].
Rudravistāra is taught as to, sa, bha, gā, and sa, ma, jā, go, ga;

338.m:
His innate bliss is manifested occasionally;
that manifestation of his is called consciousness-delight-aesthetic flavour (caitanya-camatkāra-rasa).
[Page 205]

Expansion of the mind through joy and the like is called laughter (hāsa);
[Page 206]
they call fear (bhaya) the distress of mind caused by seeing strange things and the like.

350.m:
kīlālapau, and somapaḥ, O somapāḥ, give to the soma-drinker (somape);
:p 244
with somapābhyām, from somapābhyaḥ, somapaḥ, the family of somapaḥ;
